  "title": "Phantom Limbs and Cortical Remapping",
  "emoji": "🔄",
  "color": "#7E22CE",
  "description": "A short lesson to help you apply Phantom Limbs and Cortical Remapping.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "An amputee reaches for a cup of coffee with an arm that no longer exists and feels pain in fingers that are gone.",
      "visual": "👻"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "When a limb is lost, the brain region that controlled it does not go silent. It gets invaded by neighboring regions.",
      "visual": "🗺️"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "V. Ramachandran showed touching an amputee's face could produce sensations in the missing hand due to adjacent cortical maps.",
      "visual": "😮"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Phantom Limbs and Cortical Remapping shows that brain function is more adaptive and surprising than it seems.",
      "visual": "🪞"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "The phantom limb phenomenon proves that body experience lives in the brain, not in the body itself.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "You feel it because your brain says it does.",
      "visual": "✋"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Why do amputees feel sensations in missing limbs?",
    "options": [
      "Neighboring brain regions invade the vacated cortical area",
      "The severed nerves continue sending signals",
      "The spinal cord regenerates the lost connections"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
